    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The ribosome, far from being a constitutive molecular machine, exerts\r\ndevelopmental control as apparent in the prevalence of ribosomopathies that manifest in remarkably specific developmental defects. However, much is unknown regarding the mechanism of how the ribosome affects and interacts with developmental processes. In order to further understand this role of the ribosome, we investigated a specific ribosomal protein, RPS6, deficient mouse model that presented with limb defects. Using skeletal staining and in situ hybridization with cartilage markers, we first characterized the defect as exhibiting radial underdevelopment (hypoplasia and aplasia). In order to assess if ribosomal protein reduction led to a perturbation of patterning, we explored candidate pathways and genes involved with proximal distal and anterior posterior patterning by performing in situ hybridization with candidate genes.\r\nWe identified perturbations in two genes important for limb patterning and development, namely Gli1 and Shh, indicating that ribosomal protein reduction may be interacting with these pathways. Finally, we sought to establish, beyond merely correlation, if candidate genes and pathways genetically interact with the phenotype of radial hypoplasia and aplasia. In order to do this, we reduced Shh expression and assessed if the phenotype is rescued or amplified. We found that reducing Shh expression resulted in a more severe mutant phenotype in the RPS6 deficient mouse, ascertaining the genetic interaction of RPS6 reduction and the Shh pathway. Thus, we concluded that ribosomes play a role in limb development, indicating that ribosomes are involved with the general process of patterning and development. We investigated the unknown mechanisms through which ribosomes regulate development, thus providing a potential foundation for understanding how ribosomal defects can manifest in tissue specific phenotypes.",
